<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Makhachkala</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Petrovsk-Port</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Makhachkala is the capital of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, on the western shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. It grew from the Russian fortress of Petrovskoye, founded in 1844 near the old shamkhal seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarki">Tarki</a>, and was renamed in 1921 after the revolutionary Makhach Dakhadayev. It is the largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/makhachkala.jpg" alt="Makhachkala" width="330" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suleymannabiev · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Махачкала_на_фоне_Тарки-Тау.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Makhachkala stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> and has been a major Russian seaport and a transport hub. Its historic predecessors were the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarki">Tarki</a> and Anji, or Anji-kala, which according to some sources date back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> times; some chronicles suggest Anji-kala was the name of a citadel of the Khazar capital Semender. Anji was a port town in Kumykia and part of the possessions of the Tarki state, known as the Kumyk capital from the 8th century. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumyks">Kumyks</a> the place was still called Anzhi-Qala, the Pearl Fortress, after the Russian town was founded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> was long contested. Muslim armies arrived under the Umayyad Caliph Hisham, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> in 796, and the region later fell under Seljuk, Mongol, Timurid, Safavid and, between 1578 and 1606, Ottoman influence. From the 17th century Iranians, Russians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> struggled for it. In the 1840s, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> seized the Kumyk plateau, the fort of Petrovskoye was founded at Anji-kala, named for Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>, who had waged war in the region in 1722. The fortress received town status in 1857 and was renamed Petrovsk-Port. A railway linked it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladikavkaz">Vladikavkaz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> in 1894.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> two British Royal Air Force squadrons were based at Petrovsk in 1919 and bombed Bolshevik forces. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took the city in March 1920. On 14 May 1921 it was renamed Makhachkala, after the Dagestani revolutionary Magomed-Ali Makhach Dakhadaev, and became the capital of the new Dagestan Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic. An earthquake on 14 May 1970 did major damage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Makhachkala is the capital of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> and has 623,254 residents. It is ethnically very diverse: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a>, Kumyk, Dargin, Lezgin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lak">Lak</a> nations each make up a substantial part of the population, with Russians a minority. Oil refining, mechanical engineering and textiles are the main industries, and the city is a centre of education, research and the regional press. Its commercial port handles oil, grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and other cargo, and it is linked by rail to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> network.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Makhachkala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1642, as the Kazikumukh Shamkhalate split, the shamkhals moved their seat from the Lak mountains to Tarki, on the Caspian plain near modern <b>Makhachkala</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/shamkhalate-of-tarki">Shamkhalate of Tarki</a></li></ul></section>
